<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Garden of the Hesperides</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Garden of the Hesperides was in Greek myth the garden of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a> at the western edge of the world, where golden apples were guarded by the nymphs called Hesperides and the dragon Ladon. Fetching the apples was one of the labours of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a>. Some ancient writers placed the garden near the Greek city of Euesperides, modern Benghazi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/garden-of-the-hesperides.jpg" alt="Garden of the Hesperides" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Burne-Jones · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GardenHesperides_BurneJones.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
